#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Moderate the live 0.26g server from your machine: who registers, who signs in
(with addresses), who shares an address, bans, chat mutes, closing sign-ups.

  release/moderate registrations [--since 2h] [-n 20] [--follow]
  release/moderate joins         [--since 30m] [-n 20] [--follow]   sign-ins and refused ones
  release/moderate watch [--all] [--every 60]                       suspicious sign-ups and sign-ins, as notices
  release/moderate who <name | address | range>                    accounts like that name (fuzzy), who shares their address
  release/moderate bans                                             banned accounts and addresses
  release/moderate ban <name | address | range> <hours | forever> [--reason R] [--with-ip] [--tell]
  release/moderate unban <name | address | range> [--with-ip]
  release/moderate mute <name> <hours | forever> --reason R         the player is told the reason
  release/moderate unmute <name>
  release/moderate signups [open | closed]                          no argument: the current state
  release/moderate reset-password <name>                            its next sign-in sets the new one
  release/moderate give <name> <item name | id> [count]
  release/moderate money <name> [--silver=+N] [--gold=-N] --reason R

Lines are plain text, one row each, times in UTC; --json gives one JSON object a line. A sign-up
or sign-in that looks like a banned player back starts with "!! <why> !!" (red on a terminal,
not with NO_COLOR; --json: "suspicious"): BANNED IP <address or range>, IP of banned <login>
(a banned account's last address), name like banned <login> (the names' letters without digits
or e-mail domain: the same, reversed, one inside the other, or a letter or two apart). Changes
take --dry-run: the request is shown and not sent, and exit 1 when the page refuses. Python 3, stdlib only (here: nix-shell -p python3).

Reads (registrations, joins, who, bans) run a small read-only Python on the live host: srp.db
opened read-only, and only the last --log-mb MB of logs/srp.log (registrations) and
logs/login.log (sign-ins), read by seek, under nice/ionice. The live host is small: keep
--follow at 30 s or more. Changes go through the admin page, POSTed by curl on the jump host
(the address in SRP_ADMIN_IPS); curl reads the URL and the form from stdin, so neither the token
nor the secret path is in any command line, and neither is ever printed.

Environment (nothing else is read):
  SKHID_JUMP        ssh target of the jump host, e.g. root@2.28.121.133
  SKHID_LIVE        ssh target of the live host as the jump host reaches it, e.g. root@64.176.67.220
  SKHID_SITE        the site, e.g. https://skhid.link                     (changes only)
  SRP_ADMIN_PATH    the admin page's secret path                          (changes only)
  SRP_ADMIN_TOKEN   the admin page's token                                (changes only)
  SKHID_LIVE_DATA   optional: srp's data folder on the live host
                    (default /var/lib/docker/volumes/srp26_srp26-data/_data)
The first three are in private/release/release.env. The admin path and token: the live
container's environment is the truth (the .env values once gave a 404). In bash:
  . private/release/release.env; export SKHID_JUMP SKHID_LIVE SKHID_SITE
  export SRP_ADMIN_PATH="$(ssh "$SKHID_JUMP" "ssh $SKHID_LIVE docker exec srp26-srp26-1 printenv SRP_ADMIN_PATH")"
  export SRP_ADMIN_TOKEN="$(ssh "$SKHID_JUMP" "ssh $SKHID_LIVE docker exec srp26-srp26-1 printenv SRP_ADMIN_TOKEN")"
In fish: set -x SRP_ADMIN_TOKEN (ssh $SKHID_JUMP "ssh $SKHID_LIVE docker exec srp26-srp26-1 printenv SRP_ADMIN_TOKEN")

What is not recorded: an account's creation time (only the "Registered new account" log line,
so older ones than the log read have none); where a successful sign-in came from, in logs
written before the release that logs it (those rows show the account's last address as last
saved, marked "last"); wrong passwords carry no address. A change prints the admin page's answer
line (e.g. "No such user." is a refusal; nothing changed). srp keeps accounts in memory and saves them every
few minutes, so a brand-new account may not be in srp.db yet ("not saved yet").
"""

